  Thus every tight non-AP row in this natural sub-14 single-swap window is an apex-denominator boundary tiler, not a covering row.
  This matches the LRC14 lesson from THM-560/S31o: exact tilers live on the q=N witness boundary; the covering branch must be closed by binding-pair margins and support-six residual control.

Tournament Analysis
  Vertices are row mechanisms, not runners:
  apex_q_deficit > complete_residue_lift > one_gap_one_collision > global_single_swap_tightness > q_covering_forced_margin > raw_single_swap_scan
  Observable: whether a tight row survives after quotienting by q-witness covering.  It does not: the quotient sends every row above to the apex q=N boundary, so it destroys no covering counterexample.
  Challenged assumption: small sporadic tilers are the prototype for the LRC14 covering obstruction.  In this window they are only the prototype for boundary artifacts; the covering prototype is S108's AP-drop repair slice with positive binding-pair margins.
